What do argc and args[] mean?

These allow your program to accept command-line arguments when it is run.

    argc (argument count):

        An integer representing how many arguments were passed to the program.

        It always includes the name of the program itself as the first argument.

    args[] (argument vector):

        An array of C-style strings (char*) that holds each argument.

        args[0] is the name of the program.

        args[1], args[2], etc., are any additional command-line arguments.

Example

If you run your program like this in a terminal:

./myProgram image.bmp

Then:

    argc == 2

    args[0] == "./myProgram"

    args[1] == "image.bmp"


